    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0644" n="512" xml:id="pg512"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 E. W./6–2545</note>
                <head>No. 357<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]<anchor n="1" xml:id="d357-f1"/></seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-06-25">June 25, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum of Conversation</hi>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: United States–French Relations</p>
                <table>
                    <row>
                        <cell rendition="#top #nowrap">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell>The French Ambassador, Mr. Henri Bonnet: Acting Secretary, Mr.
                            Grew.</cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>The French Ambassador called on me this morning and took up first the request of
                    his Government, presented in the appended <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi>,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d357-f2"/> that we instruct our
                    Ambassador in Moscow to keep the French Ambassador<anchor n="3" xml:id="d357-f3"
                    /> informed of developments in the work of the tripartite Reparations
                    Commission. The Ambassador pointed out once more the interest of France in the
                    whole subject of reparations and the feeling of his Government that a French
                    representative should have been included in the Commission especially in view of
                    the great sufferings which France had endured during the war. I said to the
                    Ambassador that I would immediately give consideration to his request and that
                    in the meantime he was well aware of the position we had taken that we would
                    like to have France included in the Commission and that our position in
                    principle had not altered.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> J[<hi rendition="#small-caps">oseph</hi>] C. G[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">rew</hi>] </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
